WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.362
A listening socket is an exposure decision.

2
00:00:02.362 --> 00:00:09.194
Binding to loopback can make a service local-only, while binding to a
broader interface can make it reachable from other hosts.

3
00:00:09.194 --> 00:00:15.898
Ports, firewall rules, container networking, host networking, and load
balancer configuration all shape who can connect.

4
00:00:15.898 --> 00:00:20.558
Development listeners and production services should not share casual
assumptions.

5
00:00:20.558 --> 00:00:26.560
Every listening socket should have a clear owner, purpose, exposure model,
access model, and monitoring path.

6
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.199
Connection acceptance and backlog behavior affect availability.

7
00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:38.882
A server that accepts unlimited connections or lets idle clients remain
forever can exhaust file descriptors, memory, worker threads, or event-
loop capacity.

8
00:00:38.882 --> 00:00:45.458
Backlog settings, per-client timeouts, connection limits, and graceful
close behavior should be chosen deliberately.

9
00:00:45.458 --> 00:00:51.204
The server should treat resource management as part of security because
availability is one of the system?

10
00:00:51.204 --> 00:00:52.481
s security properties.

11
00:00:52.481 --> 00:00:55.610
Authentication should happen before sensitive actions.

12
00:00:55.610 --> 00:01:02.696
A peer that can open a socket should not automatically read data, change
state, run commands, or access tenant-specific resources.

13
00:01:02.696 --> 00:01:08.251
Authorization should be enforced per command or message, not only at the
moment the connection starts.

14
00:01:08.251 --> 00:01:16.232
If a long-lived connection changes state, the server should keep
authorization decisions aligned with the current peer, resource, and
operation.

15
00:01:16.232 --> 00:01:21.084
Rate limiting, throttling, and safe shutdown behavior help keep the
service manageable.

16
00:01:21.084 --> 00:01:30.852
A server should handle malformed messages, unexpected disconnects, slow
clients, and repeated failures without leaking sensitive details or
leaving shared state inconsistent.

17
00:01:30.852 --> 00:01:42.600
Operational logs should show connection lifecycle, safe peer identifiers
where appropriate, protocol errors, authentication outcomes, and resource
pressure without turning raw payloads into permanent records.
